Q: Shall I use macros or variadic templates to manage output? A: <p>With C++11 and beyond, you can write functions like:</p>
<pre><code>template &lt;typename... Args&gt;
void error(Args&amp;&amp;... args)
{
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; &quot;[ERROR] &quot;;
    (std::cerr &lt;&lt; ... &lt;&lt; args); // fold expression
    std::cerr &lt;&lt; std::endl;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This approach gives you:</p>
<p>✔ Compile-time type checking — no unexpected macro expansions<br />
✔ Safe parameter forwarding (no comma issues)<br />
✔ Easy to extend for other message levels (<code>info</code>, <code>warn</code>, etc.)<br />
✔ Better tooling support (e.g., IDE refactorings, debugger awareness)</p>
<p>You can even namespace them instead of putting them in a class:</p>
<pre><code>namespace Log
{
    template &lt;typename... Args&gt;
    void error(Args&amp;&amp;... args) { … }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and then call:</p>
<pre><code>Log::error(&quot;User not found: &quot;, userId, '\n');
</code></pre>
<p>This reads very naturally, and performs well.</p>

Q: XPages, how to validate the number of entries in a multi-value field/control? A: <p>You can use your own validator. You will set up your field something like this</p>
<pre><code>&lt;xp:inputTextarea id=&quot;inputAddress&quot; value=&quot;#{User.user.address}&quot; disableClientSideValidation=&quot;true&quot;
    required=&quot;#{User.newEnvAgentRequest}&quot;&gt;
    &lt;xp:this.attrs&gt;
        &lt;xp:attr name=&quot;rows&quot; value=&quot;3&quot;&gt;&lt;/xp:attr&gt;
        &lt;xp:attr name=&quot;placeholder&quot; value=&quot;Enter street, no. etc... - use 2-3 lines&quot;&gt;&lt;/xp:attr&gt;
    &lt;/xp:this.attrs&gt;
    &lt;xp:this.validators&gt;
        &lt;xp:validator validatorId=&quot;userValidator&quot;&gt;&lt;/xp:validator&gt;
        &lt;xp:validateRequired message=&quot;You must enter your address&quot;&gt;&lt;/xp:validateRequired&gt;
    &lt;/xp:this.validators&gt;
&lt;/xp:inputTextarea&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>As you can see you can mix with the builtin validators (e.g. required here).</p>
<p>Then you define your validator in a been. You need to add it to the faces-config.xml (or use annotations if you use the XPages J2EE by Jesse Gallagher):</p>
<pre><code>  &lt;validator&gt;
    &lt;validator-id&gt;userValidator&lt;/validator-id&gt;
    &lt;validator-class&gt;dk.dtu.aqua.catchlog.bean.UserBean&lt;/validator-class&gt;
  &lt;/validator&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Place it eg. after all your managed-bean tags.</p>
<p>The validator class must implement the Java.faces.validator.Validator interface like:</p>
<pre><code>public class UserBean extends BaseBean implements Serializable, Validator {
:
:
</code></pre>
<p>The interface will ask you to implement a <code>validate</code> method:</p>
<pre><code>    public void validate(FacesContext context, UIComponent component, java.lang.Object value) {
        Util.trace(&quot;validator called: &quot; + component.getId() + &quot;=&quot; + value);
        if (&quot;inputName&quot;.equalsIgnoreCase(component.getId())) {
            userFacade.validateName((String) value);
        }
</code></pre>
<p>and inside of that you can identify the component from the XPage that it gets triggered for. I have called a method in my facade layer as the validation can also be triggered from service calls. But I am sure you get the idea:</p>
<pre><code>    public void validateAddress(String address, boolean isEnvAgentRequest) throws ValidatorException {
        if (isEnvAgentRequest) {
            if (StringUtil.isEmpty(address) | address.length() &lt; 5) {
                throwValidationError(&quot;Du skal angive din adresse, hvis du gerne vil være Vandmiljøagent&quot;);
            }
        }
    }
</code></pre>

Q: GRID CSS - How to center children when grid-column is defined? A: <p>This would make the trick</p>
<pre><code>._grid4 {
  display:grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
  gap:20px
}
.child {
  grid-column:span 3;
}
._grid4:has(&gt; div:first-child + div:last-child) .child:first-child {
  grid-column-start:4;
  grid-column-end:7;
}
</code></pre>

Q: Eclipse: How to load a huge class/interface (including 40000+ static fields) in a background thread A: <p>It's possible the classloader is blocked trying to perform another operation on the UI thread. Perhaps try loading the large class with another classloader to avoid blocking</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>URL[] urls = new URL[] { new File(&quot;c:/path/to/myjar.jar&quot;).toURI().toURL() };
ClassLoader classloader = new URLClassLoader(urls);
Class&lt;?&gt; intfType = classLoader.loadClass(&quot;My_Super_Big_Interface&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Note: This will have issues where a class loaded by one classloader is not equal to the same class loaded by the other classloader.</p>
